Title:
Visual Studio Extensions Revisited

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post examines Visual Studio (not VS Code) extensions as a practical initial access and execution vector, motivated by recent real-world extension supply-chain incidents. It shows how to build a modern `VisualStudio.Extensibility` extension (out-of-process, .NET 8) that triggers automatically via editor listeners (e.g., on file open) rather than user-invoked commands. The author demonstrates turning a benign formatter into a remote assembly loader that fetches Base64-encoded .NET payloads over HTTP and executes them via reflection and `AssemblyLoadContext`, yielding code execution inside `ServiceHub.Host.Extensibility.*.exe`. It then evaluates Marketplace publishing controls and finds publisher identity validation and security review to be weak, enabling straightforward publication of suspicious functionality in a VSIX. Finally, it describes an automated static-analysis pipeline (Marketplace API enumeration → VSIX unpack → IL decompile → heuristic clustering → LLM triage) and highlights at least one extension with strong backdoor-like behavior (HTTP C2 + command execution via `cmd.exe`). Useful for red teams and security researchers assessing developer-tooling supply chain risk, and for defenders hunting extension installation/execution artifacts.

Technical Focus:
- Visual Studio `VisualStudio.Extensibility` model (out-of-process extensions) vs classic VSSDK (`devenv.exe`)
- Editor event triggers via `ITextViewOpenClosedListener` / document filters
- Remote .NET assembly loading/execution (Base64 over HTTP, reflection, `AssemblyLoadContext`)
- VSIX packaging, installation flow, and Marketplace publishing process
- Large-scale static analysis of extensions (Marketplace Gallery API, `ilspycmd` decompilation, heuristic behavior clusters)
- LLM-assisted triage of decompiled code for suspicious capability alignment

Use Cases:
- Establishing stealthy execution in developer environments via Visual Studio extension triggers
- Supply-chain simulation: publishing/compromising extensions to gain distributed footholds
- Defensive hunting for VSIX installs (Temp drop paths, installer command-line artifacts)
- Building static-analysis pipelines to audit extension ecosystems at scale
- Identifying suspicious extension behaviors (telemetry vs backdoor/C2 patterns)

Keywords:
Visual Studio, Visual Studio Marketplace, VSIX, VisualStudio.Extensibility, VSSDK, devenv.exe, ServiceHub.Host.Extensibility, .NET 8, ITextViewOpenClosedListener, DocumentFilter, AssemblyLoadContext, reflection, remote assembly loader, Base64 payload, HttpClient, ilspycmd, ICSharpCode.Decompiler, extensionquery API, supply chain attack, cmd.exe execution